Unfortunately, it doesn't work yet.  It doesn't seem to break anything,
either.  The drive will be detected, and the motor will spin on command,
so it seems some commands are getting to the drive.  Data transfers
(reads, writes) don't work at all, however.

I am fairly well convinced the issue is with isadma buffer registration: 
specifically, buffers of the wrong size (or no size) allocated and 
passed to the interrupt handlers.  I haven't successfully tracked it 
down yet, though.

I'm hopeful that I will get some time soon to complete this isa_dma work, 
but someone else is welcome (encouraged, invited?) to beat me to it...
since isadma will bring support for quite a few devices with little 
additional effort.  

Jay

